25–40 mins: Coding Exercise (live or discussion-based)
Coding Question (Python + Spark logic)
Prompt

“You receive a dataset of transactions with the following fields:

user_id, transaction_id, amount, currency, transaction_ts
Remove duplicate transactions

For each user, compute total spend per day

Return only users whose daily spend > 1000”

Option A: Ask for PySpark (preferred)
Expected structure

from pyspark.sql import functions as F
from pyspark.sql.window import Window

df_dedup = df.dropDuplicates(["transaction_id"])

df_daily = (
    df_dedup
    .withColumn("date", F.to_date("transaction_ts"))
    .groupBy("user_id", "date")
    .agg(F.sum("amount").alias("daily_spend"))
    .filter(F.col("daily_spend") > 1000)
)

Follow-ups (this is where seniority shows)
“How would this behave with data skew?”

“How would you handle late-arriving data?”

“Batch vs streaming — what changes?”

Alternative SQL question (if Spark is shaky)
“Write a SQL query to get the latest record per user from a table with duplicates.”

Expected:

ROW_NUMBER() window function

Not correlated subqueries

